/**
 * \page events Acquiring Event Data 📋
 *
 * \tableofcontents
 *
 * \section events_overview Overview
 *
 * AqNWB provides the \ref AQNWB::NWB::EventsTable "EventsTable" type for recording
 * discrete events (such as TTL pulses, licks, rewards, stimulus onsets, or other
 * event-based data), where each event is anchored at a single timestamp.  An optional duration
 * column can be added when the length of each event is known.  Additional user-defined
 * columns can be appended to store any per-event metadata.
 *
 * Each \ref AQNWB::NWB::EventsTable "EventsTable" should hold events of a **single type or related
 * types** so that all rows share the same set of columns.  Events types with different metadata needs
 * (e.g., behavioral events and TTL pulses) should be stored in separate
 * \ref AQNWB::NWB::EventsTable "EventsTable" instances.
 *
 * AqNWB supports two complementary acquisition patterns:
 *
 * | Pattern | When to use |
 * |---------|-------------|
 * | **Row-based** (`addRow` / `addRows`) | Events arrive one at a time during acquisition; the total count is not known in advance. |
 * | **Column-based** (write full column vectors) | All event data are available in memory at once and can be written in a single bulk call per column. Add additional complete columns after acquisition is completed. |
 *
 * Both patterns share the same setup steps (I/O, NWBFile, table creation, and 
 * `startRecording`) and differ only in how data are written to the table.
 * The two patterns are not exclusive and hybrid approaches are possible, 
 * but require caution. In particular when using column-based append, we must 
 * ensure that all columns have been approbriately updated such that 
 * all columns have indeed the same number rows before we use `addRow` or
 * `addRows` to avoid inconsistent data. 
 *
 * \par
 * \note
 * Chunked storage is **required** for when the total number of rows is not known in advance
 * and we need to expand the columns at runtime. The  `rowChunkSize` parameter of the 
 * `createDefaultDataSpecs` controls how many rows are stored per HDF5 chunk. In practice,
 * the number of rows per chunk should typically be the same for all columns of a table. 
 * The chunking behavior can also be customized via the individual `DataSpec` of the columns. 
 * Avoid creating too small chunks (e.g,. single rows) as well as too large chunks 
 * (e.g., millions of rows per chunk).
 *
 * \section events_setup Setup: I/O, NWBFile, and table creation
 *
 * \subsection events_io 1. Create the I/O object
 *
 * \snippet tests/examples/testEventsWorkflowExamples.cpp example_events_rowbased_io_snippet
 *
 * \subsection events_nwbfile 2. Create the NWBFile
 *
 * \snippet tests/examples/testEventsWorkflowExamples.cpp example_events_rowbased_nwbfile_snippet
 *
 * \subsection events_configure_table 3. Configure and create the EventsTable
 *
 * Before creating the table, build a list of column specifications using
 * \ref AQNWB::NWB::EventsTable::createDefaultDataSpecs "EventsTable::createDefaultDataSpecs".
 * This factory returns the default set of columns (always including the `id` and
 * `timestamp` columns) and allows you to decide to include the optional `duration` and `annotation`
 * columns and control the HDF5 chunk size used for each column.
 *
 * To add a **custom column** to the table, create a
 * \ref AQNWB::NWB::VectorData::DataSpec "VectorData::DataSpec" (or a spec for any
 * other \ref AQNWB::NWB::VectorData "VectorData" subtype) and push it onto the spec
 * vector before passing it to
 * \ref AQNWB::NWB::NWBFile::createEventsTable "NWBFile::createEventsTable".
 * The spec bundles the column name, dataset configuration (data type, initial size,
 * chunk size), and description in one object. Once the spec list is ready, pass it to
 * \ref AQNWB::NWB::NWBFile::createEventsTable "NWBFile::createEventsTable" together
 * with the table name, description, and an optional source description:
 *
 * \snippet tests/examples/testEventsWorkflowExamples.cpp example_events_rowbased_create_table_snippet
 *
 * \note 
 * If you do not require custom columns (or want to create them later via 
 * \ref AQNWB::NWB::EventsTable::addColumn "addColumn)", then you can bypass the 
 * creation of the table configuration and instead configure the table directly 
 * via \ref AQNWB::NWB::NWBFile::createEventsTable "NWBFile::createEventsTable".
 *
 * \subsection events_start 4. Start the recording
 *
 * \snippet tests/examples/testEventsWorkflowExamples.cpp example_events_rowbased_start_snippet
 *
 * \note
 * When using \ref AQNWB::IO::HDF5::HDF5IO "HDF5IO", calling `startRecording` enables
 * \ref hdf5io_swmr "SWMR mode" by default.  No new datasets or groups can be added
 * after this point unless the file is closed and reopened.
 *
 * \section events_rowbased Pattern 1: Row-based acquisition
 *
 * Use this pattern when events are detected one at a time during an ongoing recording
 * session and the total event count is not known in advance.
 *
 *  \subsection events_rowbased_addrow Add rows 
 *
 * Call \ref AQNWB::NWB::DynamicTable::addRow "addRow" to append a single event, or
 * \ref AQNWB::NWB::DynamicTable::addRows "addRows" to append several events at once.
 * Each row is expressed as a
 * \ref AQNWB::NWB::DynamicTable::RowData "DynamicTable::RowData", which is an
 * `std::unordered_map<std::string, CellValue>` that maps column names to typed scalar
 * values.  Row IDs are auto-generated (0, 1, 2, …) when not explicitly supplied.
 *
 * \snippet tests/examples/testEventsWorkflowExamples.cpp example_events_rowbased_write_snippet
 *
 * \note
 * `addRow` and `addRows` write data immediately to the underlying HDF5 datasets by
 * extending the chunked arrays one row (or batch of rows) at a time.  Call
 * `io->flush()` at any point to ensure data are moved to disk.
 *
 * \subsection events_rowbased_stop Stop the recording
 *
 * \snippet tests/examples/testEventsWorkflowExamples.cpp example_events_rowbased_stop_snippet
 *
 * \section events_colbased Pattern 2: Column-based (bulk) acquisition
 *
 * Use this pattern when all event data are collected in memory first and can be
 * written to the file in a single pass. This is common for post-acquistion or 
 * offline processing pipelines or when events are buffered and flushed at the end of a trial.
 *
* \subsection events_colbased_adddata Add data to columns
 *
 * After `startRecording`, retrieve each column object via the corresponding
 * `read*Column()` accessor on the table, then call
 * \ref AQNWB::IO::BaseRecordingData::writeDataBlock "writeDataBlock" to write the
 * full column vector in one call.  Finally, set the row IDs with
 * \ref AQNWB::NWB::DynamicTable::setRowIDs "setRowIDs".
 *
 * \snippet tests/examples/testEventsWorkflowExamples.cpp example_events_colbased_create_table_snippet
 *
 * Custom columns that were not part of the original spec list can be added after
 * table creation but **before** `startRecording()` using
 * \ref AQNWB::NWB::DynamicTable::addColumn "addColumn".  Create a
 * \ref AQNWB::NWB::VectorData "VectorData" object, initialize it with an
 * \ref AQNWB::IO::ArrayDataSetConfig "ArrayDataSetConfig", and pass it to
 * `addColumn`:
 *
 * \snippet tests/examples/testEventsWorkflowExamples.cpp example_events_colbased_addcolumn_snippet
 *
 * \snippet tests/examples/testEventsWorkflowExamples.cpp example_events_colbased_start_snippet
 *
 * Write all columns — including any custom ones — after `startRecording`:
 *
 * \snippet tests/examples/testEventsWorkflowExamples.cpp example_events_colbased_write_snippet
 *
 * \subsection events_colbased_stop Stop the recording
 *
 * \snippet tests/examples/testEventsWorkflowExamples.cpp example_events_colbased_stop_snippet
 *
 * \section events_meanings Annotating columns with a MeaningsTable
 *
 * When an \ref AQNWB::NWB::EventsTable "EventsTable" column stores **coded
 * values** (e.g., integer event-type codes, stimulus IDs, or category labels),
 * a \ref AQNWB::NWB::MeaningsTable "MeaningsTable" can be attached to that
 * column to document what each code means.  The MeaningsTable lists every
 * possible value together with a human-readable description, making the data
 * self-documenting and easier to interpret.
 *
 * \subsection events_meanings_setup Setup: create the EventsTable with a coded column
 *
 * First, create the \ref AQNWB::NWB::EventsTable "EventsTable" as usual and
 * include the column whose values you want to annotate.  In this example we
 * add an integer `event_type` column:
 *
 * \snippet tests/examples/testEventsWorkflowExamples.cpp example_events_meanings_create_table_snippet
 *
 * \subsection events_meanings_create Create the MeaningsTable
 *
 * Before calling `startRecording()`, call
 * \ref AQNWB::NWB::DynamicTable::createMeaningsTable "createMeaningsTable"
 * on the \ref AQNWB::NWB::EventsTable "EventsTable" (which is a
 * \ref AQNWB::NWB::DynamicTable "DynamicTable") and pass the name of the
 * column to annotate.  The method automatically:
 *
 * - creates the \ref AQNWB::NWB::MeaningsTable "MeaningsTable" at the path
 *   `meanings_tables/<columnName>_meanings` inside the table group,
 * - adds `value` and `meaning` columns with the correct data type, and
 * - links the \ref AQNWB::NWB::MeaningsTable "MeaningsTable" back to the
 *   target \ref AQNWB::NWB::VectorData "VectorData" column.
 * 
 * Since \ref AQNWB::NWB::MeaningsTable "MeaningsTable" is a subclass of 
 *  \ref AQNWB::NWB::DynamicTable "DynamicTable", it supports the same 
 * approaches to add custom columns via `addColumn`
 * as well as the same row- and column-based write patterns described above.
 *
 * \snippet tests/examples/testEventsWorkflowExamples.cpp example_events_meanings_create_meanings_table_snippet
 *
 * \subsection events_meanings_start Start the recording
 *
 * \snippet tests/examples/testEventsWorkflowExamples.cpp example_events_meanings_start_snippet
 *
 * \subsection events_meanings_write_events Write event data
 *
 * Write the event rows to the \ref AQNWB::NWB::EventsTable "EventsTable"
 * using `addRow` / `addRows` as usual:
 *
 * \snippet tests/examples/testEventsWorkflowExamples.cpp example_events_meanings_write_events_snippet
 *
 * \subsection events_meanings_write_meanings Write meanings data
 *
 * Then populate the \ref AQNWB::NWB::MeaningsTable "MeaningsTable" by writing
 * the `value` and `meaning` columns.  All possible values of the coded
 * column may be listed, even if they do not appear in the recorded data.
 * Since meanings only list values, we do not need to record the events first,
 * i.e., if the meanings data is known beforehand, then we can also populate the 
 * MeaningsTable before writing the data to the main table.
 *
 * \snippet tests/examples/testEventsWorkflowExamples.cpp example_events_meanings_write_meanings_snippet
 *
 * \subsection events_meanings_stop Stop the recording
 *
 * \snippet tests/examples/testEventsWorkflowExamples.cpp example_events_meanings_stop_snippet
 *
 * \section events_tips Tips and best practices
 *
 * - **One table per event type.** Keep event types with different metadata, e.g., lick events, 
 *   TTL, or reward deliveries in separate \ref AQNWB::NWB::EventsTable "EventsTable" instances so
 *   that every row in a table shares the same column schema.
 * - **Choose the right chunk size.** For row-based acquisition, a `rowChunkSize` of
 *   100–1000 may be a good trade-off between write and read
 *   performance.  Larger chunks reduce overhead when reading but can waste space if the
 *   chunks are not aligned well with rows of the table. 
 * - **Avoid many small write operations:** Depending on the number and rate of events, 
 *   it may be useful to collect multiple rows in memory when using row-based acquisition 
 *   in order to use `addRows` to write larger blocks of data to avoid many 
 *   small I/O operations and repeated updates to the same chunks in the file. 
 * - **Customize the column layout.** Call
 *   \ref AQNWB::NWB::EventsTable::createDefaultDataSpecs "EventsTable::createDefaultDataSpecs"
 *   to obtain the default spec vector, modify individual entries (e.g., change the
 *   chunk size of a specific column), and pass the modified vector to
 *   \ref AQNWB::NWB::NWBFile::createEventsTable "NWBFile::createEventsTable".
 * - **Add custom columns.** After table creation (but before `startRecording`), call
 *   \ref AQNWB::NWB::DynamicTable::addColumn "DynamicTable::addColumn" to attach
 *   additional \ref AQNWB::NWB::VectorData "VectorData" columns for any per-event
 *   metadata not covered by the built-in columns.
 * - **Document coded columns with a MeaningsTable.** When a column stores integer
 *   codes or other enumerated values, call
 *   \ref AQNWB::NWB::DynamicTable::createMeaningsTable "createMeaningsTable"
 *   before `startRecording` to attach a
 *   \ref AQNWB::NWB::MeaningsTable "MeaningsTable" that maps every possible code
 *   to a human-readable label. You may list **all** possible values in the MeaningsTable,
 *   even if a particaly allowed value may do not appear in the recorded data.
 */
